architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "0009"
  title: Split and compact subcommands for multi-document files
  status: proposed
  date: "2026-08-23"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  context: |
    A SYON file can hold more than one document. Block 2 fences carry a
    path.format info string precisely so an embedded document can say where
    it belongs and what it is, and the AST already models the result as
    SyonFile.documents, a vector of Document with path, format and body.

    Nothing consumes that. There is no way to turn a multi-document file into
    the tree of files it describes, and no way to gather a tree back into one
    file. The glossary corpus under examples/ is a directory of separate
    .syon files precisely because the alternative does not exist yet. The
    obvious pair of operations is split, which writes each embedded document
    to its own path, and compact, which walks a directory and emits a single
    multi-document file.

    Auditing the current implementations against that goal found four gaps.
    All of them are load-bearing, and none is visible from the AST alone.

    First, the Rust parser parses fence bodies as SYON. The spec does not:
    spec/03-semantics.md states that the parser does NOT parse embedded
    content and returns it as a raw string, and Document.body is typed as a
    parsed Value, which cannot hold one. A fence declaring .json and holding
    real JSON is rejected outright with SYON-003. Splitting such a fence back
    out to a .json file is therefore impossible today, and this is the whole
    point of the format field. ADR 0006 recorded this as a known open gap.

    Second, the Go parser keeps fence bodies raw, conforming to the spec, but
    accepts only one fence per file. A second fence is rejected with
    SYON-003. So one implementation supports many documents with bodies it
    should not be reading, and the other reads bodies correctly but supports
    one document.

    Third, the two implementations disagree on how to split the info string.
    Go takes the last dot, matching the spec's statement that the part after
    the last dot is the format. The Rust grammar's path_char excludes dots,
    so it takes the first. For an info string of a.b.json, Go yields path a.b
    and format json, Rust yields path a and format b.json. Those reconstruct
    to different filenames, so a round trip through split would not be
    stable across implementations.

    Fourth, the CLI can only ever show the first document. Given a file with
    two fences it prints one and silently drops the rest, so there is no way
    to observe the multi-document behaviour that already exists.

    A fifth finding pointed the same way and was small enough to fix while
    writing this record, so it is no longer outstanding. A --- inside a |
    block scalar body was rejected with SYON-117, although the body is
    verbatim text and the marker is content; a -- or a ---- in the same
    place was kept, correctly, and syon-go accepted all three. The preflight
    scan was giving marker-like text structural meaning in a place where it
    has none, which is the exact mistake a splitter would repeat if it went
    looking for boundaries itself. It now tracks block scalar extents the
    way it tracks fences, and both implementations agree.

    There is also a safety question with no current answer. A fence path is
    attacker-controlled text inside a data file. A split that writes to it
    naively is a directory traversal primitive, which is a poor thing to
    build into a format whose name begins with Safe.
  decision: |
    Add two subcommands, syon split and syon compact, as inverse operations
    over the document set, and fix the four gaps above as prerequisites
    rather than working around them.

    syon split FILE writes each document to its path, rooted at a target
    directory given by an option and defaulting to the current directory.
    syon compact DIR walks that directory and emits one multi-document file.
    The contract is on the document set, not on bytes: compact after split
    reproduces the same documents with the same paths, formats and bodies.
    Byte-identical output for the host document is not promised, because
    comment placement and blank lines are not fully recoverable.

    Fence bodies are carried verbatim in both directions. Split writes the
    bytes the fence held. Compact embeds the bytes the file held. Neither
    converts anything: a format of json is a label on the content, not an
    instruction to serialise SYON into JSON, and compact does not parse the
    file it embeds. This keeps split and compact total over any embedded
    content, including formats SYON knows nothing about.

    Split may run a level 0 check, a shallow scan that looks for text
    resembling a document boundary without being one and reports what it
    finds. The check is advisory in the strict sense: it never changes which
    documents are produced, never changes their content, and never changes
    the exit status. It writes a warning, and the split proceeds exactly as
    it would have without it.

    Only a well-formed fence pair opens and closes a document. A line of --
    or ---- resembles a separator and is not one, so it never becomes a file
    of its own. It stays verbatim in the document that contains it, which is
    the one that began before it. The same holds for a --- or a ... sitting
    inside a raw fence body, where the surrounding content is not SYON at
    all and those bytes carry no SYON meaning, and for any of them inside a
    block scalar body, which the fifth finding above already settled at the
    parser level. The check exists so an author
    who typed one dash too few hears about it, not so the splitter can guess
    what they meant.

    A file may hold at most one unfenced document, the host. Split writes it
    to a reserved name in the target directory, and compact reads that name
    back as the host rather than treating it as one more embedded file, so
    the host survives the round trip instead of being dropped or demoted.

    Fence paths are untrusted input and are validated before anything is
    written, rejecting rather than sanitising, consistent with how the
    forbidden set is handled elsewhere. A path is rejected when it is
    absolute, when any segment is a parent reference, when it normalises to
    a location outside the target directory, when a component on the way to
    it is a symbolic link, or when it is empty. An anonymous document has no
    location to be written to and is an error under split rather than
    something to invent a name for. Each rejection gets its own code in the
    2xx fence band defined by ADR 0008.

    The prerequisites, in order:

    Document.body gains the ability to hold raw bytes, so a fence body is
    returned unparsed as spec/03-semantics.md already specifies, and the
    Rust parser stops parsing fence content. This is the change that makes a
    non-SYON fence representable at all.

    The Go parser accepts any number of fences per file.

    The Rust grammar splits the info string at the last dot, matching Go and
    the spec. This is a behaviour change for any info string containing more
    than one dot.

    The CLI gains a way to address documents beyond the first, so the
    multi-document model is observable without writing files.

    One question is deliberately left open: whether compact should refuse to
    embed a file it cannot read as valid content for its declared format.
    Refusing would make compact a validator, accepting keeps it total, and
    the argument depends on use cases that do not exist yet.
  consequences:
    positive:
      - The path and format fields finally do something. They have been carried through the grammar, the AST and both implementations without a consumer, which is why the divergences between implementations went unnoticed for so long.
      - Splitting forces fence bodies to be raw, which is what the spec always said and what the Go implementation always did. The conformance gap gets closed by a feature that cannot work without it, rather than by a cleanup nobody schedules.
      - A corpus like examples/glossary can be distributed as one file and worked on as a tree, or the reverse, without a bespoke script on either side.
      - Keeping the level 0 check advisory means the tree split produces is a function of the fence structure alone, so it can be reasoned about without knowing which heuristics fired. A warning that could move a byte from one output file to another would be a parser, not a warning.
      - Path validation gives the fence a documented threat model. Writing files from a data format is where a safe subset earns its name, and the rules are stated once rather than reinvented per caller.
    negative:
      - Changing the info-string split to the last dot is a breaking change for any document whose path contains a dot. Nothing in this repository relies on the current Rust behaviour, but the grammar is published.
      - Raw fence bodies change Document.body's type, which is a breaking API change for every consumer of the Rust AST, including the Python bindings and the FFI surface.
      - Compact must decide a traversal order for the directory, and that order is part of the output. Sorted by path is the only defensible choice, but it means a compacted file does not preserve the order documents had before a split.
      - Two more subcommands is two more surfaces to keep in step across implementations, and ADR 0004 chose independent implementations knowing that cost.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: Convert content on the way through, honouring the format field
      reason: Rejected because it makes split lossy and compact partial. A fence declaring toml would need a TOML serialiser to write and a TOML parser to read back, so every format in the wild becomes a dependency, and any format SYON lacks a codec for becomes unsplittable. Carrying bytes verbatim is total and needs nothing.
    -
      name: Sanitise unsafe fence paths rather than rejecting them
      reason: Rejected for the same reason the forbidden set rejects rather than rewrites. Silently turning a parent reference into something safe means the file on disk no longer matches the document that described it, and the round-trip contract is the first casualty.
    -
      name: Provide a library API only, with no CLI subcommands
      reason: Rejected because the multi-document model is currently unobservable, and that is a large part of why its implementations drifted apart. A subcommand is what makes the behaviour testable from the corpus in CI, the way examples-valid already tests parsing.
    -
      name: Use YAML's own multi-document markers instead of fences
      reason: Rejected because the marker forms are in the forbidden set. ADR 0005 established that a document using a fence is not YAML-parseable anyway, so the fence is already the SYON-only mechanism for this, and adding a second one would mean two answers to the same question.
    -
      name: Let the level 0 check fail the split, or treat a marker-like line as a boundary
      reason: Rejected because it makes the output depend on a heuristic. A file either has fences or it does not, and a splitter that sometimes split on -- would produce a different tree than the parser's own document set, which is what the round-trip contract is stated over. A warning that changes behaviour is not a warning, and a check that can reject is a second, weaker validator competing with the parser.
    -
      name: Drop the unfenced host document on split
      reason: Rejected because the host is where a real corpus keeps the mapping that gives the embedded documents meaning. Losing it on every round trip would make split useful only for files that happen not to have one.
